---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="astronomy"
  topic="eclipses"
  description="Interactive eclipse simulator: move the Moon and the line of nodes, see the umbra and penumbra, and find out when total, annular, partial and lunar eclipses happen and why not every month."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={eclipseQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      For a few minutes the sky darkens, birds fall silent and the Sun becomes a black disc ringed with pearly light. A
      total solar eclipse is one of nature’s most spectacular sights, yet it needs the Sun, Moon and Earth to line up
      almost perfectly. Move the Moon, follow its shadow, and discover why eclipses are rare and why they come in seasons.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <EclipseSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Drag the Moon in the <strong>top view</strong>, or use the slider, to move it to <strong>New moon</strong> (solar eclipse) or <strong>Full moon</strong> (lunar eclipse). The circle on the right shows what you would see from Earth.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>angle between the Sun and the Moon’s nodes</strong>. Watch the <strong>side view</strong>: when the nodes point away from the Sun, the Moon passes above or below the shadows.</li>
      <li>Switch the Moon’s <strong>distance</strong> between Near, Average and Far, and compare total and annular eclipses.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Varies</strong>, then press <strong>Start</strong> to run time forward. The list records every eclipse; notice how they cluster into eclipse seasons.</li>
      <li>Click any view or body to learn more. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the starting arrangement.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="shadows">Shadows in space</h2>
  <p>
    The Sun lights Earth and the Moon from one side, so each casts a long shadow into space. Because the Sun is a wide disc
    rather than a point, every shadow has two parts. In the <strong>umbra</strong>, the dark core, the Sun is completely
    hidden. In the surrounding <strong>penumbra</strong>, only part of the Sun is hidden. An eclipse happens when one
    body moves into the other’s shadow.
  </p>

  <h2 id="solar">Solar eclipses</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>solar eclipse</strong> happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth, at new moon. There are three
    main types:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Total:</strong> the Moon completely covers the Sun. The sky darkens and the Sun’s faint outer atmosphere, the corona, appears. Totality is seen only inside the Moon’s umbra, a path on Earth usually less than about 270 km wide, and lasts at most about 7½ minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Annular:</strong> the Moon is near the far part of its orbit and looks slightly smaller than the Sun, so a bright “ring of fire” remains. The umbra ends before it reaches Earth.</li>
    <li><strong>Partial:</strong> the Moon covers only part of the Sun, as seen from places in its penumbra, or when the alignment is not exact.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Total eclipses are possible because of a coincidence: the Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon but also about
    400 times farther away, so both appear about half a degree wide.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lunar">Lunar eclipses</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>lunar eclipse</strong> happens when the Moon passes into Earth’s shadow, at full moon. In a
    <strong>total</strong> lunar eclipse the whole Moon is in the umbra and often turns coppery red: sunlight passing
    through Earth’s atmosphere is bent into the shadow, and the blue part is scattered away, the same reason sunsets are
    red. In a <strong>partial</strong> eclipse only part of the Moon enters the umbra; in a <strong>penumbral</strong>
    eclipse it only grazes the penumbra and dims so slightly that many people do not notice. A lunar eclipse can be seen
    from everywhere on Earth’s night side and can last for hours.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-not">Why not every month?</h2>
  <p>
    There is a new moon and a full moon every 29.5 days, but eclipses are much rarer. The reason is that the Moon’s orbit
    is <strong>tilted about 5°</strong> to the plane of Earth’s orbit around the Sun. The two places where the Moon’s
    orbit crosses that plane are called <strong>nodes</strong>. At most new moons the Moon passes slightly above or below
    the line from Earth to the Sun, and its shadow misses Earth; at most full moons it passes above or below Earth’s
    shadow. Five degrees sounds small, but at the Moon’s distance it is about ten Moon-widths.
  </p>

  <h2 id="seasons">Eclipse seasons</h2>
  <p>
    An eclipse needs a new or full moon that falls close to a node. That only happens when the line of nodes points
    roughly towards the Sun, which occurs twice a year, about 173 days apart. Each of these <strong>eclipse
    seasons</strong> lasts a little over a month, long enough to contain at least one new moon, so there is at least one
    solar eclipse each season, often with a lunar eclipse two weeks before or after. Every year has between four and
    seven eclipses in total. The line of nodes slowly turns, so the seasons drift earlier each year, and similar eclipses
    repeat after about 18 years and 11 days, a period called the <strong>saros</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <h2 id="safety">Watching safely</h2>
    <p>
      Never look directly at the Sun, even when it is mostly covered, and never through binoculars, a telescope or a camera
      without a proper solar filter. Use certified eclipse glasses (meeting the ISO 12312-2 standard) or project the Sun’s
      image through a pinhole. Only during the brief total phase of a total solar eclipse is it safe to look without
      protection. Lunar eclipses are completely safe to watch.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Umbra</dt>
    <dd>The darkest part of a shadow, where the light source is completely blocked.</dd>
    <dt>Penumbra</dt>
    <dd>The outer part of a shadow, where the light source is only partly blocked.</dd>
    <dt>Node</dt>
    <dd>One of the two points where the Moon’s orbit crosses the plane of Earth’s orbit.</dd>
    <dt>Corona</dt>
    <dd>The Sun’s hot, faint outer atmosphere, visible during a total solar eclipse.</dd>
    <dt>Saros</dt>
    <dd>A cycle of about 18 years 11 days after which a similar eclipse repeats.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Eclipses in science and history</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Testing relativity:</strong> during the 1919 total eclipse, starlight bending around the Sun was measured, supporting Einstein’s theory of gravity.</li>
    <li><strong>Discovering helium:</strong> the element was first identified in 1868 from the spectrum of the Sun’s edge during an eclipse.</li>
    <li><strong>Earth is round:</strong> Aristotle noted that Earth’s shadow on the Moon during lunar eclipses is always curved.</li>
    <li><strong>Dating history:</strong> eclipses recorded in ancient texts help historians pin down exact dates.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The phases of the Moon are caused by Earth’s shadow.”</strong> Phases come from our viewing angle; Earth’s shadow only matters during a lunar eclipse.</li>
    <li><strong>“Eclipses happen every month.”</strong> The 5° tilt makes them happen only in eclipse seasons.</li>
    <li><strong>“A partially covered Sun is safe to look at.”</strong> Even a thin crescent can damage your eyes.</li>
    <li><strong>“A total solar eclipse is visible everywhere it is daytime.”</strong> Totality is only seen along a narrow path.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The eclipse type is calculated from real values: the sizes of the Sun, Earth and Moon, their distances, and the
    5.145° tilt. For solar eclipses the view is from the best-placed spot on Earth, found by allowing for the Moon’s
    parallax; for lunar eclipses Earth’s shadow is enlarged by 2% for the atmosphere, as eclipse predictors do. The
    drawings are not to scale: Earth, the Moon and the heights in the side view are enlarged eight times so you can see
    them, while the Earth–Moon distance is to scale. The Moon’s orbit is drawn as a circle, and the “Varies” setting uses
    a simple sine-wave model of its changing distance. Real eclipse prediction also accounts for the Moon’s elliptical,
    perturbed orbit, Earth’s rotation and its shape, so this simulator does not predict real dates or paths.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Solar eclipses happen at new moon, when the Moon’s shadow falls on Earth; lunar eclipses at full moon, when the Moon enters Earth’s shadow.</li>
    <li>Shadows have a dark umbra and a paler penumbra; their sizes decide whether an eclipse is total, annular, partial or penumbral.</li>
    <li>The Moon’s 5° orbital tilt means eclipses only happen when a new or full moon falls near a node.</li>
    <li>Eclipses come in seasons about 173 days apart, with four to seven eclipses each year.</li>
    <li>Never look directly at the Sun without certified eye protection.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
